# Review

## Summary
The paper studies the generalization properties of Random Feature (RF) regression, which is equivalent to performing ridge regression on the features $\Phi(x)$ produced by a random mapping $g(w, x)$. The authors show that the test MSE monotonically decreases with the number of features and the number of samples, which implies that it is better to have as many features as possible. The second part of the paper studies the optimal value of the ridge parameter and the "fitting ratio" $\mathcal{R}_{tr/te}$, which is defined as the ratio between the training MSE and the test MSE. The main result (Theorem 2) shows that for kernel ridge regression (KRR) with power-law eigenstructure, there exists a small constant $r_*^2$ such that the optimal fitting ratio $\mathcal{R}_{tr/te}^*$ is equal to $r_*^2 + O(n^{-\gamma})$, where $\gamma = \min(1, 2\alpha + 1 - \beta)$. In particular, this implies that $\mathcal{R}_{tr/te}^* \to 0$ when $n \to \infty$, which means that overfitting is obligatory. The paper also provides experimental evidence that several image datasets of practical interest satisfy the power-law assumption.

